.TH NSHELL 1 "2026-10-26" "nshell development" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nshell \- an interactive shell with an S-expression command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nshell
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-i | \-\-interactive ]
.RB [ \-l | \-\-login ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-config ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-history ]
.RB [ \-\-private ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR SHELL_COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]]
.RB [ \-\-command
.IR COMMAND
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nshell
provides real-time syntax highlighting, history-aware autosuggestions,
inline abbreviations, and context-aware completion.
.PP
When started with standard input connected to a terminal,
.B nshell
runs an interactive read-eval-print loop. Otherwise it reads commands from
standard input in batch mode. With
.BR \-c ,
it delegates the command and its trailing arguments to
.BR /bin/sh .
With
.BR \-\-command ,
it executes a single nshell
.I COMMAND
and exits.
.PP
This manual describes the default branch. The published v0.6.6 release
predates the S-expression command language documented here.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-c " SHELL_COMMAND " "[ARGS...]"
Delegate
.I SHELL_COMMAND
and its trailing arguments to
.BR /bin/sh .
.TP
.BI \-\-command " COMMAND"
Execute
.I COMMAND
once in nshell batch mode and exit with its status.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Load the login environment before starting nshell or delegating a command.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Force the interactive line editor, even when standard input is not a
terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not load the interactive startup file.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Load
.I PATH
instead of the default
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nshell/init.lisp
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is absolute, or
.I ~/.config/nshell/init.lisp
otherwise. With the default path, a legacy
.I ~/.nshellrc
is ignored with a one-line notice.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-history
Do not load or save the history file, or record commands in in-session history.
.TP
.B \-\-private
Unless
.B \-\-no\-history
is also set, an interactive session reads existing history without saving its
commands. Private mode also suppresses local assistant transcript, snapshot,
and audit persistence.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
The default Emacs-style bindings use \fBC\-\fR for Ctrl
and \fBM\-\fR for Alt (Meta).
\fBC\-a\fR moves to the start of the line; \fBC\-e\fR moves to the end,
or accepts the autosuggestion when already at the end.
\fBM\-b\fR moves back one word; \fBM\-f\fR moves forward one word,
or accepts the next suggested word at the end of the line.
\fBRight\fR and \fBC\-f\fR move forward one character,
or accept the entire autosuggestion at the end of the line.
.PP
\fBC\-k\fR cuts to the end of the line; \fBC\-u\fR cuts to the start;
\fBC\-w\fR cuts the preceding word; \fBC\-y\fR reinserts the last cut text.
\fBC\-_\fR undoes the last edit.
.PP
\fBC\-r\fR lists the matching history entries under the prompt.
\fBC\-r\fR/\fBC\-s\fR and the arrow keys move the selection,
.B Enter
runs the selected entry, and
.B ESC
restores the line that was being typed.
.PP
Setting the environment variable
.B NSHELL_VI_MODE
to a non-empty value other than 0, false, or no (case-insensitive)
enables vi key bindings at startup. Use
.B set -o vi
or
.B set +o vi
at an interactive prompt to enable or disable them during the session. Press
.B ESC
to enter normal mode, which supports motions (\fBh l 0 ^ $ w b e\fR), insert
entries (\fBi a I A\fR), edits (\fBx D C s r\fR), character finds
(\fBf F t T\fR, repeated with \fB;\fR or \fB,\fR), and \fBgg\fR/\fBG\fR
for input positions. Counts combine with motions and operators. The \fBd\fR,
\fBc\fR, and \fBy\fR operators work with motions and text objects, including
\fBiw\fR/\fBaw\fR and quoted or paired text. Use \fBp\fR/\fBP\fR to put text,
\fBu\fR to undo, \fBC\-r\fR to redo, and \fB.\fR to repeat the last change.
The \fBv\fR command starts character-wise visual selection, and \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR
navigate history.
.PP
Press \fBM\-e\fR (\fBAlt\-E\fR) to open the current line in the external editor
selected by \fBNSHELL_EDITOR\fR, \fBVISUAL\fR, or \fBEDITOR\fR, falling back to
\fBvi\fR; the edited line is returned to the prompt without being executed.
.PP
On macOS, configure the terminal's Option key to send Meta or an Escape prefix
if Alt bindings do not register.
.PP
Press \fBC\-x\fR to request a captured rerun of the previous command.
Review the complete command and safety classification, then press the displayed
confirmation binding again to execute it. Long commands wrap without truncation.
The rerun is refused if the confirmation cannot fit, contains control
characters, or the command is blocked or requires a live terminal.
Any other key or a terminal resize cancels the confirmation. This executes
the command again; it does not replay saved output.
.PP
The
.B bind
builtin lists, sets, erases, and resets these bindings; run
.B bind
with no arguments to see the current table. In vi mode, the cursor changes
shape with the mode: a block in normal mode, a bar while inserting. Disabling
vi mode restores the terminal's default cursor shape.
.SH SYNTAX
An unparenthesized line uses shell-command syntax. A parenthesized list with
a simple symbol head is a Common Lisp host form when its head resolves to a
host form; otherwise, the head names a command and the list is a command
island. Command-island arguments are quoted as command values by default.
.PP
Use \fB$NAME\fR to pass a shell variable's value as an argument, and
\fB,EXPR\fR to evaluate a Common Lisp expression and pass its value. String
literals stay literal; value escapes do not interpolate into string text.
.RS
.EX
(set example "preserved")
(echo $example ,(+ 2 3))
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every evaluated form has value, stream, or test position. Value position
returns a Lisp value. Stream position reads or writes bytes. A direct command
in test position uses its exit status as a boolean.
.PP
In bare-line input, \fB|\fR connects byte streams. In an S-expression pipeline,
\fB(| ...)\fR connects byte streams and \fB->\fR passes structured values
between internal commands and transformations. For example:
.RS
.EX
(-> '((:state "stopped" :id 7 :command "demo")
      (:state "running" :id 8 :command "other"))
    (where (= :state "stopped"))
    (select :id :command))
.EE
.RE
.PP
The value library provides \fBto-json\fR/\fBfrom-json\fR,
\fBto-tsv\fR/\fBfrom-tsv\fR, and \fBto-lines\fR/\fBfrom-lines\fR for
converting between Lisp values and JSON, TSV, or line text. It also provides
\fBregex-match-p\fR, \fBregex-find\fR, and \fBregex-replace\fR. The
\fBstring match -r\fR builtin also matches regular expressions.
.SH HISTORY EXPANSION
Interactive bare-line input supports history designators \fB!!\fR (the previous
command), \fB!$\fR (the previous command's last argument), \fB!-\fIN\fR (the
command \fIN\fR entries back), \fB!?\fItext\fB?\fR (the most recent command
containing \fItext\fR), and \fB!\fIprefix\fR (the most recent command beginning
with \fIprefix\fR). An unresolved designator reports an error instead of being
parsed or stored. Exclamation marks in single quotes or preceded by a
backslash remain literal.
.SH CONTROL FLOW
S-expression input supports Common Lisp control forms including \fBif\fR,
\fBwhen\fR, \fBunless\fR, \fBcond\fR, \fBcase\fR, and \fBdotimes\fR. A direct
command used as a conditional test is evaluated in test position, where its
exit status supplies the boolean result.
.SH FUNCTIONS AND ERRORS
Define shell functions with \fBdefun\fR:
.RS
.EX
(defun greet (name) (echo $name))
(greet "hello")
.EE
.RE
.PP
A function can be defined inside a control form. When a function call resolves
to a name not yet loaded, nshell checks
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nshell/functions/NAME.nsh\fR when
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is absolute; otherwise it checks
\fB~/.config/nshell/functions/NAME.nsh\fR. The file must define the requested
function with \fBdefun\fR.
.PP
A command failure in a function can signal \fBcommand-failed\fR, which
\fBhandler-case\fR can handle. At an interactive prompt, a failed function
call offers the \fBIgnore and continue\fR restart; choosing it skips the failed
command and continues the remaining forms in the function. Pressing Ctrl-C
returns control to the prompt.
.SH BUILTINS
.B nshell
provides the following builtins:
.BR . ,
.BR [ ,
.BR alias ,
.BR abbr ,
.BR agent ,
.BR ai ,
.BR and ,
.BR bg ,
.BR bind ,
.BR break ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR contains ,
.BR continue ,
.BR count ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR disown ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fg ,
.BR function ,
.BR functions ,
.BR help ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR math ,
.BR nextd ,
.BR not ,
.BR or ,
.BR pipeline\-graph ,
.BR popd ,
.BR prevd ,
.BR printf ,
.BR prompt ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR seq ,
.BR set ,
.BR source ,
.BR string ,
.BR test ,
.BR theme ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR unset ,
.BR wait ,
.BR which .
Run
.B help
inside the shell for details.
.SH ASSISTANT
The
.B agent
builtin is interactive-only. Every proposed command, including one classified
as safe, requires
.B Enter
before execution. Blocked commands cannot be approved.
.PP
The assistant refuses to start if its sidecar reports tools or MCP servers
outside the permitted initialization contract. Use
.B ai status
to inspect assistant state and
.B ai set
to change session settings.
.SH HISTORY FILE
The default path is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nshell/history
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is absolute, or
.I ~/.local/share/nshell/history
otherwise. The file is created with owner-only permissions. Symlinks, hard
links, nonregular files, and files owned by another user are rejected.
.PP
If the default file does not exist, nshell imports records from the legacy
.I ~/.nshell_history
file. It leaves that source unchanged, deduplicates imported command text,
and reads the new file on later starts.
.PP
Malformed or truncated records are reported. Complete records before damaged
data remain readable, and the reader resumes at a later valid record frame
when one is present. On the next successful append, nshell repairs the file
with valid unique records, adds the command if it is not already present, and
discards damaged data.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-no\-history
to disable history reads and writes, including in-session history recording.
Use
.B \-\-private
to keep existing history available without saving this session's commands;
unless
.B \-\-no\-history
is also set. Private mode also suppresses local assistant transcript, snapshot,
and audit persistence.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
An absolute path selects
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nshell/init.lisp
as the default interactive startup file. If unset, empty, or relative, nshell
uses
.IR ~/.config/nshell/init.lisp .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
An absolute path selects
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nshell/history
as the history file. If unset, empty, or relative, nshell uses
.IR ~/.local/share/nshell/history .
.TP
.B NSHELL_VI_MODE
When set to a non-empty value other than 0, false, or no (case-insensitive),
enables vi key bindings at startup. The interactive commands
.B set -o vi
and
.B set +o vi
enable and disable them during a session.
.TP
.B NSHELL_GREETING
Printed at interactive startup in place of the version banner. An empty value
starts the session silently. Read after the selected startup file runs, so
that file can set it.
.TP
.B NSHELL_PROMPT
Sets the left prompt's format string at session start. See
<https://nerima-lisp.github.io/nshell/reference/builtins/>.
A
.B prompt left
line in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nshell/init.lisp
or the default
.I ~/.config/nshell/init.lisp
overrides it.
.TP
.B NSHELL_RIGHT_PROMPT
As
.BR NSHELL_PROMPT ,
for the right prompt.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a nonempty value, disables colored output.
.TP
.B COLORTERM
Set to
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
to select 24-bit color; otherwise the depth is read from TERM.
.TP
.B HOME
Used for tilde expansion.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for external commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a one-off command:
.RS
.EX
nshell \-\-command 'string upper hello'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Iterate over a generated sequence:
.RS
.EX
(dotimes (i 5) (echo ,i))
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B nshell
exits with the status of the last command executed, or 0 on a clean
interactive exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fish (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR zsh (1)
